# Response headers.
#
# This file is the single source of the response headers, and the assets layer
# applies it to everything it serves. It replaced the [[headers]] blocks in
# netlify.toml, which went with the rest of the Netlify configuration: a config
# the host ignores is worse than none, because the file that looks like the
# configuration stops being the one that is served.
#
# The syntax is the `_headers` convention: a path pattern, then its indented
# headers. `*` matches within a segment; `/fonts/*` covers the directory.

# The font filenames are stable, NOT content-hashed (fonts/inter.woff2), so a
# long immutable cache would pin a client to a replaced file for a year with no
# way to invalidate it. Thirty days keeps the win and bounds the mistake: to
# ship a new font, rename the file.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# Security headers.
#   * X-Frame-Options + frame-ancestors 'none' - the portal is never iframed.
#   * script-src 'self' - no inline script anywhere in the page, which is also
#     why the theme bootstrap lives in theme-init.js instead of a <script> tag.
#     Note what this does and does not stop. Netlify used to inject its own HUD
#     into served HTML as `/.netlify/scripts/hud`; Cloudflare injects nothing,
#     so the badge and the two console errors it caused went with the move.
#     Nothing needs an allowance for it here.
#   * connect-src is 'self' only. The page fetches exactly one thing over the
#     network, /api/watchlist, and it is same-origin. It used to also allow
#     *.netlify.app for the per-row reachability probes; those went with the
#     badge, so the allowance went too rather than lingering as a hole that
#     nothing needs.
#   * frame-src names the three dashboards that can actually be framed, and
#     names them individually rather than by a wildcard. A wildcard is how this
#     directive once became permission for every *.netlify.app subdomain to
#     serve three known hosts — the same reasoning that closed connect-src,
#     applied to the other directive that had one.
#
#     The three dashboards moved from Netlify to Cloudflare Workers (their old
#     *.netlify.app origins are gone), and this list moved with them. Irish
#     Visa Tracker is absent on purpose: it sends X-Frame-Options: DENY, so
#     the page never frames it (a row there explains that instead of opening a
#     box).
#
#     This list and the manifest's `embed` flags have to agree, and the audit
#     enforces it: an entry with embed !== false whose origin is missing here
#     would be blocked by OUR policy while looking like the dashboard's fault.
#     If a dashboard moves again, both places change together.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://contenttrackerdashboard.pages.dev https://rbiweeklydashboard.pages.dev https://ucdcsnlmodulepicker.pages.dev; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'

# Self-hosted webfonts. These are the files a returning visitor otherwise
# re-requests on every visit: unversioned names, a few hundred KB together, and
# content that changes only when the typeface does. A week plus
# stale-while-revalidate trades that round trip for a copy that can still be
# replaced within a week — deliberately not `immutable`, because a promise made
# on a stable URL cannot be revoked. The catch-all above sets no Cache-Control on
# purpose: Cloudflare joins the Cache-Control of every matching rule, so a
# `no-store` there would silently cancel this one. Same rule RBI and the Content
# Tracker already carry.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=86400

# The two vendored stylesheets (normalize + Open Props). Same reasoning as the
# fonts above: stable content behind stable names.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=86400
